  "result": "Reservations with expiry are in, and `npm test` passes (175 tests, including 10 new ones).\n\n**Store (`src/store.js`)**\n- It has `reserve`, `getAvailable`, `confirm` and `release`.\n- `removeItem` now throws `INSUFFICIENT_STOCK` when the quantity is more than what's available.\n- Reservations expire when `clock() >= creation time + ttlMs`, using only the store's clock.\n- Expired reservations stay in memory so `confirm` can answer `RESERVATION_EXPIRED` instead of not-found. They are never cleaned up, so memory grows slowly if many expire unconfirmed.\n- Ids are counter-based strings like `res-1`.\n- `confirm` returns the SKU, which the API uses to build its response.\n\n**API (`src/api.js`)**\n- `GET /stock/:sku` also returns `available`.\n- `POST /reservations` returns 201 `{id}`, 409 for `INSUFFICIENT_STOCK`, and 400 for any other error, which in practice means `INVALID_QTY`.\n- `POST /reservations/:id/confirm` returns 200 `{sku, stock}`, 410 for an expired reservation, and 404 for anything else.\n- `DELETE /reservations/:id` always returns 204.\n\n**Tests:** New cases are in `test/store.test.js` and `test/api.test.js`. They cover:\n- the exact expiry boundary\n- confirming twice\n- idempotent release\n- `removeItem` against reserved stock\n- every status code\n\nI didn't add validation for `ttlMs`, since the spec doesn't mention it. A missing or non-numeric value would make the expiry time `NaN`, so the reservation would count as active forever.",
